# Route Loading (`loading.tsx`)

Use Next.js `loading.tsx` + shared loader components for **segment suspense** while server content loads.

## Components

| Component | Path | Use in |
|-----------|------|--------|
| `RouteLoading` | `@/components/loaders/route-loading` | `loading.tsx` files |
| `PanelRouteLoading` | `@/components/loaders/panel-route-loading` | Admin panel segments |
| `FullPageLoader` | `@/components/loaders/full-page-loader` | Root/auth full viewport |

## Variants

```tsx
// Root or auth — full viewport
export default function Loading() {
  return <RouteLoading variant="full-page" label="Loading…" />;
}

// Admin panel — centered in content area below header
export default function AdminLoading() {
  return <RouteLoading variant="panel" label="Loading page…" />;
}
```

## When

- Add `loading.tsx` next to `page.tsx` when the page has async server work and should show a fallback.
- **Panel routes:** prefer shared **skeletons** from `@/components/loaders/skeletons` (see `.cursor/rules/loaders-skeletons.mdc`) instead of a centered spinner.
- Use `variant="panel"` only for legacy fallbacks; new work should use `Panel*PageSkeleton`.
- Use `variant="full-page"` for public/auth/root segments.

## Do not

- Put business logic in `loading.tsx`.
- Use route loading for button/action pending states — use `InlineSpinner` instead.
- Add a new panel center spinner when a skeleton template fits.
